Let  $E$  be a point such that  $DOEC$  is a parallelogram. It follows that  $OE = DC = AB$  and  $OE \parallel DC \parallel AB$ , which implies that  $OABE$  is a parallelogram. Thus, we have \begin{align*}
\angle OCE &= \angle COD 
&= 180^{\circ} - \angle AOB 
&= 180^{\circ} - \angle OBE
\end{align*}
which means that  $OBEC$  is a cyclic quadrilateral. It follows that  $\angle OBC = \angle OEC = \angle ODC$ , as desired.  $\blacksquare$

We will prove given inequality by induction.

For  $ n_{0} \equal{} 2$  we have
\[ \frac {1}{n_{0} \plus{} 1} \left( 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2n_{0} \minus{} 1} \right) \equal{} \frac {1}{3} \left( 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \right) \equal{} \frac {4}{9} \textrm{,}
\]
which is greater than
\[ \frac {1}{n_{0}} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2n_{0}} \right) \equal{} \frac {1}{2} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \right) \equal{} \frac {3}{8} \textrm{.}
\]
Now, we will assume
\[ \frac {1}{k \plus{} 1} \left( 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \minus{} 1} \right) > \frac {1}{k} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \right) \textrm{,}
\]
for some  $ k \in \mathbb{N}$ ,  $ k \geqslant 2$ , and try to prove
\[ \frac {1}{\left( k \plus{} 1 \right) \plus{} 1} \left( 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2 \left( k \plus{} 1 \right) \minus{} 1} \right) > \frac {1}{\left( k \plus{} 1 \right)} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2 \left(k \plus{} 1 \right)} \right) \textrm{,}
\]
which is equivalent to
\[ 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \minus{} 1} \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 1} > \frac {k \plus{} 2}{k \plus{} 1} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 2} \right) \textrm{.}
\]
By assumption we have
\[ 1 \plus{} \frac {1}{3} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \minus{} 1} \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 1} > \frac {k \plus{} 1}{k} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \right) \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 1} \textrm{,}
\]
so it is sufficient to show
\[ \frac {k \plus{} 1}{k} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \right) \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 1} > \frac {k \plus{} 2}{k \plus{} 1} \left( \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \plus{} \frac {1}{2k \plus{} 2} \right) \textrm{.}
\]
Last inequality is equivalent to
\[ \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} > \frac {k^{2}}{2 \left( k \plus{} 1 \right) \left( 2k \plus{} 1 \right)} \textrm{,}
\]
which is true according to extended AM-HM inequality
\[ \frac {1}{2} \plus{} \frac {1}{4} \plus{} \ldots \plus{} \frac {1}{2k} \overset{AM\minus{}HM}{ > } \frac {k^{2}}{2 \plus{} 4 \plus{} \ldots \plus{} 2k} \equal{} \frac {k^{2}}{k \left( k \plus{} 1 \right)} > \frac {k^{2}}{2 \left( k \plus{} 1 \right) \left( 2k \plus{} 1 \right)} \textrm{.}
\]
Q. E. D.

