Let the rectangular array be  $A = (a_{ij}).$  Let  $M_i$  be the row sum of  $i$ th row for  $1 \leq i \leq m$  and  $N_j$  be the column sum for  $j$ th row for  $1\leq j \leq n.$  Furthermore, we have  $M_i > 0$  and  $N_j > 0.$  The problem statement says that whenever  $a_{ij} >0$  for some  $i, j$  then  $M_i = N_j.$  We need to show  $m=n.$ If  $M_i = N_j$  whenever  $a_{ij} >0,$  we have  $\frac{a_{ij}}{M_i} = \frac{a_{ij}}{N_j}$  for all  $i,j.$  Now, \begin{align*}
    \sum_{i, j} \frac{a_{ij}}{M_i} &= \sum_{i,j} \frac{a_{ij}}{N_j}  \implies \sum_{i=1}^m \left(\frac{1}{M_i} \sum_{j=1}^n a_{ij}\right) &= \sum_{j=1}^n \left(\frac{1}{N_j} \sum_{i=1}^m a_{ij}\right)  \implies \sum_{i=1}^m \left(\frac{1}{M_i} M_i\right) &=  \sum_{j=1}^n \left(\frac{1}{N_j} N_j\right)  \implies \sum_{i=1}^m 1 &= \sum_{j=1}^n 1  \implies m &= n. 
\end{align*}

The minimum is not very interesting (it's simply zero), so we focus more on the maximum.

The idea is to double count angles between two edges that are oppositely oriented. Let there be  $x$  directed triangles and  $y$  non-directed triangles.

On one hand, for every vertex, there are at most  $n^2$  angles, because we have  $k(2n-k)$  angles where  $k$  is the number of edges facing outwards. Thus, there are at most  $n^2(2n+1)$  angles. On the other hand, every directed triangle contributes three angle, while every non-directed triangle contributes one angle. Furthermore, there are  ${2n+1 \choose 3}$  triangles in total. Thus, we have the system of equations
\begin{align*}
x+y &= {2n+1 \choose 3} 
3x + y &\leq n^2(2n+1).
\end{align*}
This solves to obtain at most  $x=\frac{n(n+1)(2n+1)}6$  directed triangles.

