If square  $ABCD$  has side length  $4$  and square  $AEFG$  has side length  $x$ , then in  $\Delta ADG$  we have: $|DG| = \sqrt{x^2-16}$  (i); $\angle{AGD} = \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{4}{\sqrt{x^2-16}}\right)$  (ii); $\angle{DGF} = \frac{\pi}{2}-\angle{AGD}$  (iii).

If  $\Delta DFG$  has area  $1$ , then we have: $\frac{1}{2}|FG||DG|\sin \angle{DGF} = \frac{x\sqrt{x^2-16}}{2}\sin\left[\frac{\pi}{2}- \sin^{-1}\left(\frac{4}{\sqrt{x^2-16}}\right)\right] = \frac{x\sqrt{x^2-16}}{2}\cos\left[\sin^{-1}\left(\frac{4}{\sqrt{x^2-16}}\right)\right]=1$ ;

or  $\frac{x\sqrt{x^2-16}}{2}\cos\left[\cos^{-1}\left(\frac{\sqrt{x^2-32}}{\sqrt{x^2-16}}\right)\right]=1$ ;

or  $\frac{x\sqrt{x^2-32}}{2} = 1 \Rightarrow x^4-32x^2-4=0 \Rightarrow x^2 = \frac{32\pm \sqrt{32^2-4(1)(-4)}}{2} \Rightarrow x^2 = 16 \pm 2\sqrt{65}$ .

Since we require the area of  $AEFG$  to be positive, we ultimately have  $\textcolor{red}{x^2=16+2\sqrt{65}}$ .

