Suppose that  $x,y,z$  are positive real numbers that satisfy
\begin{align*}
x+y+z&=xyz
\frac{x^2}{16(1+x^2)}=\frac{y^2}{25(1+y^2)}&=\frac{z^2}{36(1+z^2)}.
\end{align*}
Compute  $$ \frac{x^2(1+x^2)^2}{x^2(1+z^2)^2}. $$

For some integers  $b$  and  $c$ , neither of the equations below have real solutions:
\begin{align*}
2x^2+bx+c&=0
2x^2+cx+b&=0.
\end{align*}
What is the largest possible value of  $b+c$ ?

Let  $\alpha$  and  $\beta$  be the two roots of  $x^2+2017x+k$ . What is the sum of the possible values of  $k$  so that the lines 
\begin{align*}
y&=2\alpha x+2017^2
y&=3\alpha x+2017^3
\end{align*}
are perpendicular?
